DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The ggeettsseerrvveenntt__rr(), ggeettsseerrvvbbyynnaammee__rr(), and ggeettsseerrvvbbyyppoorrtt__rr()  functions
       are  the  reentrant  equivalents  of, respectively, ggeettsseerrvveenntt(3), ggeett‐‐
       sseerrvvbbyynnaammee(3), and ggeettsseerrvvbbyyppoorrtt(3).  They differ in the way  that  the
       _s_e_r_v_e_n_t  structure  is  returned, and in the function calling signature
       and return value.  This manual page describes just the differences from
       the nonreentrant functions.

       Instead of returning a pointer to a statically allocated _s_e_r_v_e_n_t struc‐
       ture as the function result, these functions copy  the  structure  into
       the location pointed to by _r_e_s_u_l_t___b_u_f.

       The  _b_u_f array is used to store the string fields pointed to by the re‐
       turned _s_e_r_v_e_n_t structure.  (The nonreentrant functions  allocate  these
       strings  in  static  storage.)   The size of this array is specified in
       _s_i_z_e.  If _b_u_f is too small, the call fails with the error  EERRAANNGGEE,  and
       the caller must try again with a larger buffer.  (A buffer of size 1024
       bytes should be sufficient for most applications.)

       If  the  function call successfully obtains a service record, then _*_r_e_‐
       _s_u_l_t is set pointing to _r_e_s_u_l_t___b_u_f; otherwise, _*_r_e_s_u_l_t is set to NULL.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On success, these functions return 0.  On error, they return one of the
       positive error numbers listed in errors.

       On error, record not found (ggeettsseerrvvbbyynnaammee__rr(),  ggeettsseerrvvbbyyppoorrtt__rr()),  or
       end of input (ggeettsseerrvveenntt__rr()) _r_e_s_u_l_t is set to NULL.

